      "name": "strncoll",
      "signature": "void strncoll(undefined4 * pGameObject)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "Safely destructs a game object and releases all associated resources using Microsoft structured exception handling.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Save current exception handler chain pointer from FS:[0]\n2. Establish new exception handling frame with handler at 0x6fc321bf\n3. Initialize cleanup state variable to 0 (cleanup not started)\n4. Calculate address of critical section at offset +0x50 from game object base\n5. Delete the critical section via g_pfnDeleteCriticalSection function pointer\n6. Set cleanup state to 0xffffffff (critical section deleted)\n7. Call DestroyGameObjectWithMembers to clean up remaining object resources\n8. Restore original exception handler chain from saved pointer\n9. Return to caller with stack cleanup (stdcall convention)\n\nParameters:\n  pGameObject - Pointer to InitializedGameObject structure to be destructed\n    - Must be a valid pointer to initialized game object\n    - Object's critical section at offset +0x50 must be properly initialized\n    - Structure size is 96 bytes (0x60)\n\nReturns:\n  void - Function does not return a value\n  - Cleans up 4 bytes on stack (1 parameter) per stdcall convention\n\nStructure Layout - InitializedGameObject:\n  Offset   Size   Field Name              Type        Description\n  ------   ----   ----------              ----        -----------\n  0x00     4      field_0                 ?           Unknown field\n  ...      ...    (various fields)        ...         ...\n  0x50     28     criticalSection         CRITICAL_SN Critical section for thread sync\n  ...      ...    (remaining fields)      ...         ...\n  0x60     96     TOTAL SIZE              -           Total structure size in bytes\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - Uses Microsoft Structured Exception Handling (/EHsc) for safe cleanup\n  - Exception handler address: 0x6fc321bf\n  - Critical section must be initialized before calling this destructor\n  - No double-delete protection; safe to call only once per object\n  - Function handles any exceptions during cleanup via SEH mechanism",
