      "name": "UnlinkObjectFromList",
      "signature": "void UnlinkObjectFromList(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "Unlinks an object from a doubly-linked list and performs cleanup.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Load the pointer parameter from the stack\n2. Add 0xc to the pointer to locate the embedded _LIST_ENTRY structure within the object\n3. Call UnlinkListNode to remove the object's list entry from the doubly-linked list\n4. Return from function\n\nParameters:\npObject (void *): Pointer to an object that contains an embedded doubly-linked list entry at offset 0xc from its base. The list entry structure (_LIST_ENTRY) contains forward and backward pointers used to maintain the list.\n\nReturns:\nvoid: No return value. Function performs in-place modification of the linked list structure.\n\nSpecial Cases:\nThis is a tail-call optimization that jumps directly to UnlinkListNode rather than using a traditional call+return. It serves as a destructor/cleanup wrapper function referenced from a function pointer table at address 0x6fc3bd34, indicating it is called when an object is being destroyed or removed from active management.\n\nThe offset 0xc indicates the object structure has:\n- Offset 0x0-0xb: Data fields (12 bytes)\n- Offset 0xc: Embedded _LIST_ENTRY structure containing forward/backward pointers",
