      "name": "UnwindExceptionVector",
      "signature": "void UnwindExceptionVector(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "C++ Exception Unwinding Thunk for Vector Destruction\n\nThis is a small exception unwinding helper function that safely destructs a vector\nof objects with C++ exception handling enabled. It is used in exception cleanup to\nproperly destruct exception-thrown objects in LIFO order. This function is typically\nreferenced from exception handler jump tables (vtable-style dispatch).\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Load pointer to vector element array from caller's stack frame (EBP + 4)\n2. Add offset 0x14 to the loaded pointer to access the actual vector element data\n3. Push the calculated address onto stack as argument\n4. Call DestructVectorWithExceptionHandler to destruct all elements with SEH support\n5. Return to caller (stdcall - callee cleans 4-byte argument)\n\nParameters:\n- pElementArray: Pointer to vector storage that needs unwinding/destruction. The actual\n  element array is located at offset +0x14 from this base pointer.\n\nReturns:\n- void (no return value)\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- The offset 0x14 (20 bytes) is added to adjust for vector header/control structure\n- This function is typically called from exception handlers or virtual destructors\n- Uses __stdcall calling convention (callee cleans stack)\n- Part of C++ exception handling infrastructure\n\nRelated Functions:\n- DestructVectorWithExceptionHandler: Does the actual element destruction with SEH",
      "name_source": "LoD/1.11",
