      "name": "EncodeItemVariantData",
      "signature": "void EncodeItemVariantData(void * pItemStruct, char * pSourceData)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "comment": "Encodes item variant data using run-length encoding into a compact format.\n\nThis function takes raw item variant data (up to 15 chunks of 32 bytes each) and\nencodes it using variable-length encoding. Null bytes are used as separators between\nchunks, and the encoding scheme compresses consecutive bytes by storing length values.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Allocate temporary encoding buffer (0x128 = 296 bytes) and initialize to zeros\n2. Loop through up to 15 chunks (0xf iterations):\n   a. Scan current chunk for null terminator (max 0x7f = 127 bytes per scan)\n   b. If length == 0x7f: store literal 0x7f byte and continue\n   c. If length < 0x7f: encode as [length_byte][count][data_bytes] format\n   d. If chunk boundary (chunkIndex >= 15): write separator bytes (0x00, 0x00)\n3. Calculate encoded data size (difference of output pointer - input pointer)\n4. Check if size fits in 0x10000 (64KB) limit\n5. Allocate final output buffer (0x169 = 361 bytes max) and initialize to zeros\n6. Copy encoded data from temporary buffer to final output buffer (in 4-byte chunks)\n7. Update item structure fields:\n   - Set flag bits at offset +0x8 (OR with 0x5)\n   - Store output buffer pointer at offset +0x10 (dword)\n   - Store encoded data size at offset +0xa (word/short)\n8. Call InitializeModule(0x176, 0) to finalize encoding\n\nParameters:\n  pItemStruct: Pointer to item structure that will store encoded data\n    Offset +0x8: Flags byte (modified with OR 0x5)\n    Offset +0xa: Word/short for encoded data size\n    Offset +0x10: Dword pointer to encoded data buffer\n  pSourceData: Pointer to source item variant data (15 x 32-byte chunks = 480 bytes)\n    Structure: 15 consecutive 32-byte chunks separated by boundaries\n\nReturns:\n  void - Modifies pItemStruct in-place with encoded data pointer and size\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  Magic numbers:\n    0x128 (296): Temporary buffer size for encoding\n    0x169 (361): Final output buffer size allocation\n    0x7f (127): Maximum scan length and run-length encoding limit\n    0xf (15): Maximum number of chunks to process\n    0x10000 (65536): Maximum encoded data size limit\n    0x5: Flag bits to OR into item structure offset +0x8\n    0x176: Module initialization code after successful encoding\n\n  Error handling:\n    If temporary buffer allocation fails: call CleanupAndAbort with code 0x129\n    If encoded size >= 0x10000: call CleanupAndAbort with code 0x166 (too large)\n    If final output buffer allocation fails: call CleanupAndAbort with code 0x16a",
